  784 create signatures exist in both sessions. Not one of them is tiled in one log and linear
  in the other, or UBWC in one and not in the other. Both sessions made all the same creates:
  the signature set is equal, and what differs is how many times the same image was recreated
  (13 against 6), which is what a session further along or back in the same area looks like.

=== 4. the first run, and the confound its numbers carried ===
The first comparison (B1 against A) left 19 creates whose signature was missing from B1, among
them four `R8_UNORM 1024x1024 listn=0 tile=tiled ubwc=0`. A 1024x1024 single-channel image is
what an overlay's font atlas looks like, and B1 was the run without MANGOHUD while A had it on.
B2, run with the overlay on, removes the whole set: `only in the first` and `only in the second`
both go to 0. That is why the gate is read from B2 and B1 is kept as the record of the confound:
an inventory log is a session, and an overlay in the session is part of what created images.
B1 also had 0 differing decisions on its 775 shared signatures.

=== 5. verdict ===
The inventory half of the `exp-21` gate passes in the form queue 3 asked for. On 784 create
signatures taken from the game itself, `exp-21` returns the same tiling and UBWC verdicts as the
shipped `0014`, class for class and create for create, and the no-list volume it returns is the
same 2.4 GiB of tiled / 1.6 GiB of linear in the same proportions. Together with
results/test-exp21-equiv-p14.log (0 differing lines of 598 on the whole rig), nothing measured
here separates the two builds.

=== 6. what this does not show ===
  * It is one game session against one game session. A create the game never reached in either
    run is not covered by either log; the two runs are the same area and timeout, not a sweep.
  * `ubwc=0` on every no-list create is the reference behaviour and the patch's behaviour alike.
    This gate says the rewrite did not change the UBWC answer; it says nothing about whether
    that answer should be different (that is the 64-bit question, journal 31 and queue 1).
  * No fps, and no claim about performance: an inventory is a list of allocations, and the wall
    this game runs into is UCHE (ADR-0008).
  * The reference column is an inventory log, not a driver under our control: build
    `050375a18b7b` was the tree with `0014` as it was released, and the binary is not in this
    repository. What we compare are decisions in two logs of the same game.
